<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><strong>The last month of Sir Keir Starmer=
=E2=80=99s premiership </strong>ended with stronger than forecast growth. N=
ew ONS data shows the UK economy expanded by 0.4% in July alone, smashing f=
orecasts. That follows growth of 0.3% in June 2026 and no growth in May 202=
6.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The ONS says July=E2=80=99s growth was bec=
ause of rises of 0.4% in services, 0.2% in production and 0.1% in construct=
ion. On an annual basis, real UK GDP is estimated to have grown by 1.3% in =
the quarter to July 2026 the ONS reports. That=E2=80=99s relatively slow by=
 historic standards, but in line with the UK=E2=80=99s weak growth over the=
 last few years.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">England=E2=80=99s sensational run to the t=
hird-fourth playoff in the men=E2=80=99s football World Cup, and the heatwa=
ve, both affected the UK economy in July, the ONS says. However, computer p=
rogramming was a big factor behind growth, as the AI boom pushed up activit=
y.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Digging into today=E2=80=99s GDP report, w=
e can see the UK services sector had a decent July, with growth in 11 of it=
s 14 subsectors. According to the ONS, the fastest growth was in: professio=
nal, scientific and technical activities; IT and communication; admin and s=
upport service activities; and manufacturing, with growth of 0.9% in the mo=
nth.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Economists are reacting favourably to the =
data. Paul Dales, chief UK economist at Capital Economics, is hopeful AI co=
uld prove to be a longer-lasting boost to real activity. Ben Jones, CBI sen=
ior lead economist, says Britain=E2=80=99s economy has been more resilient =
to the Iran war than expected.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">But Richard Carter, head of fixed interest=
 research at wealth managers Quilter Cheviot, warns that future growth will=
 be =E2=80=9Chard to come by=E2=80=9D, while Andrew Hunter, senior economis=
t at Moody=E2=80=99s Analytics, says downside risks to UK economy remain hi=
gh.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">He adds: =E2=80=9CIt is worth remembering =
the repeated pattern in recent years of strong growth in the first half of =
the year being followed by a sharp slowdown in the second.=E2=80=9D</p>
